  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracting in Arkansas is concentrated and smaller year to date. FY2026 to date obligations are $746M, spread across 13,784 awards, with an average award size of $54.1K. Obligations fell 40.9% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, with $445M obligated, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $94.6M and the General Services Administration with $47.1M. Construction and civil works lead industry spending: Commercial and Institutional Building Construction totaled $213M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction totaled $119M. A few large contractors capture meaningful shares: BL HARBERT INTERNATIONAL LLC has $102M, ALBERICI CONSTRUCTORS INC has $68.2M, and ARMTEC COUNTERMEASURES CO has $47.5M. Fiscal-year comparisons show higher past totals, including FY24 at $1.9B and FY21 at $1.5B. These figures reflect partial, fiscal year to date obligations, so totals can change as the year continues.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Arkansas changed this year?",
        "body": "FY2026 to date obligations are $746M. Obligations fell 40.9% year over year. FY24 obligations were $1.9B and FY25 obligations were $1.3B, showing that current-year totals are lower than several prior years. The award count is 13,784 with an average award size of $54.1K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries drive federal spending in Arkansas?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer with $445M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $94.6M and the General Services Administration with $47.1M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry with $213M, and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction is second with $119M. Other notable industries include Computer Facilities Management Services with $32.4M and Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction with $33.2M."
      }
    ]
  },
